 \begin{abstract}
-% Background (1 sentence).
-Contextual skills supply reusable instructions to language-model agents,
-motivating efforts to transfer the resulting internal representations
-across executions.
-% Gap / research question (1 sentence).
-When such transfer weakens at later layers, does the original execution
-also depend less on the skill source, or does the transfer omit components
-that still matter?
-% Approach (2 sentences).
-We pair skill-position residual replacement in a wrong-skill receiver with
-cumulative source-attention knockout in the correct-skill execution,
-comparing both interventions on common eligible questions across depth.
-Early content and position controls validate the transfer probe, and a
-focused experiment adds source-position key/value cache replacement.
-% Results / findings / insight (2 sentences).
-Across six verified model--benchmark comparisons involving four open-weight
-models on MedCalc-Bench and TheoremQA, transfer recovery falls below half
-of the donor's gain before source-attention dependence falls to half,
-revealing a depth interval with weak transfer but persistent dependence.
-On Qwen3-8B/MedCalc at layer $16$, adding source-cache replacement raises
-accuracy from $3.5\%$ to $42.1\%$, while a subsequent reader-to-source cut
-reduces it to $7.0\%$; this identifies a partial repair in one setting,
-without uniquely explaining late transfer failure.
+Agent skills provide task-specific capabilities by loading instructions and
+supporting resources into a language model's context. We investigate what
+allows the internal states produced from a skill to support another
+execution. Across model depth, we transplant correct-skill residual states,
+the hidden vectors passed between model blocks, into a wrong-skill
+execution. Separately, we block subsequent attention to the skill in the
+original execution. Across seven verified comparisons spanning five models from four families
+on MedCalc and TheoremQA, residual-state transfer weakens before dependence on source access
+subsides. Content, position and cross-question controls establish that early
+transfer carries structured skill information. To investigate the later
+separation, we jointly replace skill states and the source positions'
+key/value (KV) cache, which stores attention vectors for previously processed
+tokens. On Qwen3-8B/MedCalc, this raises layer-$16$ accuracy from $3.5\%$
+with residual replacement alone to $42.1\%$. Cutting subsequent reading of
+the repaired source reduces accuracy to $7.0\%$. Skill-state transfer thus
+depends on more than the replaced residual vectors: compatible source
+caches can restore part of the effect, while continued source access can
+remain necessary for the recovered behaviour. The mechanism evidence
+covers one model--task setting and identifies source caches and subsequent
+reading as concrete factors to test when reusing contextual skills.
 \end{abstract}
